course: euphemisms
lesson: 29
title: Soft words for hard things: lying and honesty and drinking and drugs
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Calling someone a liar or a drunk is rude, so people reach for softer words.
explain: Officials say a claim was "not entirely accurate" instead of "false"; businesses "massage the numbers" instead of faking them.
explain: For drinking, people joke or are polite: someone is "merry", "tipsy" or has had "one too many" instead of being plainly drunk.
explain: A famous British example: newspapers wrote that a politician was "tired and emotional" when they meant he was drunk and could not say so.
explain: Some of these words are kind or just funny, like "pulling your leg", but many hide a real lie or a real problem.
fact: "Not entirely accurate" is a polite way to say false.
fact: "Less than candid" is an official way to say dishonest; "candid" means open and honest.
fact: "Shading the truth" means lying slightly.
fact: A "misstatement" is an official word for a lie or false claim.
fact: "Fudging the figures" means falsifying (faking) numbers.
fact: To "embroider" a story means to add invented details to it.
fact: To "spin a yarn" means to tell an invented story.
fact: "Bending the truth" means lying.
fact: "Being diplomatic" means not saying what you really think, to avoid hurting or angering someone.
fact: "Pulling your leg" means lying to you as a joke.
fact: "Massaging the numbers" means changing data so it looks better than it really is.
fact: A "fish story" is a US phrase for an exaggerated or invented claim.
fact: "Tired and emotional" is a British joke meaning drunk.
fact: "Merry" in Britain means slightly drunk.
fact: "Tipsy" means slightly drunk.
fact: "Had a few" means has drunk enough alcohol to be drunk.
fact: "One too many" means drunk, having had more alcohol than one should.
fact: "Over the limit" means too drunk to drive by law.
fact: "Well-oiled" is slang for drunk.
fact: "Three sheets to the wind" is an old phrase for very drunk.
exercise: A report says a claim was "not entirely accurate". What does it mean? | The claim was untrue in parts | The claim was almost perfect | The claim was too long | The claim was in pencil
exercise: An official was "less than candid" with the court. What does it mean? | He was not fully honest | He was not sweet enough | He spoke too quietly | He was very honest
exercise: What does "shading the truth" mean? | Twisting the truth a little | Shadows in a drawing | Hiding from the sun | Speaking in the dark
exercise: A spokesman admits a "misstatement". What does he admit? | A lie or false claim | A spelling mistake | A misplaced stamp | A statement read too fast
exercise: What does "fudging the figures" mean? | Falsifying numbers | Making fudge in shapes | Counting slowly | Drawing figures of people
exercise: If you "embroider" a story, what do you do? | Add invented details | Sew the story onto cloth | Tell it in a soft voice | Shorten it
exercise: What does "spin a yarn" mean? | Tell an invented story | Make wool on a wheel | Tie a long knot | Play with a ball of string
exercise: What does "bending the truth" mean? | Lying or twisting facts | Telling the truth gently | Bending a ruler | Changing your mind
exercise: When a friend is "being diplomatic" about your painting, what are they doing? | Not saying what they really think | Working as an ambassador | Telling you their honest opinion of it | Signing a peace treaty
exercise: "I'm just pulling your leg!" What did the speaker do? | Lied to you as a joke | Pulled your leg to hurt you | Helped you stand up | Measured your leg
exercise: What does "massaging the numbers" mean? | Changing data to look better | Rubbing a sore calculator | Counting very gently | Adding up very slowly
exercise: In US talk, what is a "fish story"? | An exaggerated or invented claim | A true report of a fishing trip | A cookbook for fish | A cartoon about a fish
exercise: A British paper says a politician was "tired and emotional". What does it mean? | He had drunk too much | He was sleepy and sad | He had been crying at work | He had worked all night
exercise: In Britain, if someone at a party is "merry", what are they? | Slightly drunk | Getting married | Singing carols | Very sad
exercise: What does "tipsy" mean? | Slightly drunk | About to fall over a cliff | Leaving a tip at a cafe | Standing on tiptoe
exercise: "He's had a few." What does it mean? | He has had a few drinks | He has had a few snacks | He has had a few ideas | He has had a few lessons
exercise: "She's had one too many." What does it mean? | She has drunk too much alcohol | She ate one more cake than allowed | She has one too many coats | She scored one goal too many
exercise: What does "over the limit" mean for a driver? | Too drunk to drive by law | Driving too fast | Carrying too many passengers | Parked too long
exercise: What does "well-oiled" mean about a man? | He is drunk | He is a smooth machine | He has oily hair | He is good at his job
exercise: What does "three sheets to the wind" mean? | So drunk he can barely stand | Out sailing on a windy day | Doing the washing outside | Sleeping under three blankets
test: Gran, about the twins' drawing: "Well, it's very... colourful." Which word best fits what she is doing? | Being diplomatic | Spinning a yarn | Fudging the figures | Pulling your leg
test: "Mate, there's no homework today." "Seriously?" "Nah, just pulling your leg." Is there homework? | Yes, he was joking | No, he told the truth | Nobody knows | Only for some pupils
test: News: "The company massaged the numbers to hide its losses." What did the company do? | Changed its figures to look better | Paid a masseur | Counted its money gently | Showed its real losses honestly
test: "Dad's had one too many, so Mum's driving us home." Why is Mum driving? | Dad has drunk too much alcohol | Dad ate too much cake | Dad is too tired after a long day at work | Dad lost the car keys
test: Police: "The driver was over the limit." What did the driver do wrong? | Drove with too much alcohol in his blood | Drove faster than the speed limit | Drove with too many people | Parked over a line
test: "Course he caught a shark, it's a fish story," laughs the American aunt. What does she think? | The claim is exaggerated or made up | Her nephew caught a real shark | It is a story about a goldfish | It is a true tale from a book
test: "He embroidered the story a bit — there were two dogs, not twenty." What did he do? | Added invented details to the story | Sewed a picture of dogs | Told the exact truth | Forgot to mention the dogs
test: Report on a minister: "His answers were less than candid." What does the report say? | He was dishonest in his answers | He spoke too sweetly | His answers were too short | He was very honest
test: "Uncle Rob was proper well-oiled at the wedding, fell in the cake." What was wrong with Uncle Rob? | He was drunk | He had slipped on oil | He was a good dancer | He was tired from work
test: A newspaper writes that the MP was "tired and emotional" in Parliament. What are they really saying? | He was drunk | He needed sleep | He was crying about bad news | He gave a moving speech
test: "Grandpa's a bit tipsy after one sherry, lol." Which is true? | Grandpa is slightly drunk | Grandpa is very drunk | Grandpa has not drunk at all | Grandpa is about to tip the waiter
test: Coach: "Stop fudging the figures — you didn't run 10 laps, you ran 6." What did the runner do? | Gave false numbers | Ate fudge while running | Counted perfectly | Ran extra laps
test: "That sailor came back three sheets to the wind." How was the sailor? | Very drunk | Soaked by the rain | Carrying sails | Sleepy and cold
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the expression "shading the truth"? | It denotes lying to a slight degree | It denotes a drawing executed with shadows | It denotes the concealment of the sun | It denotes truthful speech in darkness
exam: Identify the principal meaning of "a misstatement" as acknowledged by an official spokesman. | A lie or false claim | A typographical error | An address written incorrectly | A statement delivered at excessive speed
exam: It is given that a guest at a festive gathering is described as "merry". Select the response that is consistent with the established British usage. | The guest is slightly drunk | The guest is about to be married | The guest is singing hymns | The guest is greatly distressed
exam: Ascertain the meaning of the expression "to spin a yarn". | To relate an invented story | To manufacture woollen thread | To tie a lengthy knot | To play with string
